A||||6||Ferrari_cdn||Should I cover the wire plugs?||||5||
Z||000000||Ferrari_cdn||12-01-2001||02:44 AM||||Because of the winter, I decided to unplug the "going down wire switch" for my headlights.  Unplugging those wires will leave the headlights up when I turn the lights off.  That way the headlight doors don't get frozen shut and damage the motor.  Is it allright leaving the wire plug and socket dangling or is it best to cover it with something?<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ferrari_cdn" TARGET=_blank>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_cdn.JPG">   </A>  <BR>AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||5
Z||000001||SLYGUY||12-01-2001||03:27 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ferrari_cdn:<P><B>That way the headlight doors don't get frozen shut <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Can't say that I've ever heard of something like that. I could understand moisture freezing but what fluid is in question here? Can grease freeze? Gears freeze? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> <P>And I thought living anywhere besides California was a good thing.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> <P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||12-01-2001||06:19 AM||||prob wouldnt be a bad idea so smear some white dialelectric grease in the opening.||||reg||1
Z||000003||tesmith66||12-01-2001||07:06 AM||||Beleive me, they can freeze shut and destroy the actuator!<P>I always run my finger around the opening before I turn them on if any snow or ice is present. You can also squirt them with de-icer.<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||4
Z||000004||Fierowrecker||12-01-2001||12:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ferrari_cdn:<BR><B>Because of the winter, I decided to unplug the "going down wire switch" for my headlights.  Unplugging those wires will leave the headlights up when I turn the lights off.  That way the headlight doors don't get frozen shut and damage the motor.  Is it allright leaving the wire plug and socket dangling or is it best to cover it with something?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Never had a problem with my 84 headlights...<BR>13 years of driving summer and winter, the headlights never stuck down...<BR>And the car stayed outside all the time too!<BR><P>------------------<BR>crash... The Fierowrecker<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/uploads/crash_sig.jpg">        <IMG SRC="http://fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||12-01-2001||03:52 PM||||The 87 and up Gen 2 motors won't be hurt if frozen one way or the other... the controller will shut them off.<P>The older motors might could be hurt that way.<P>The connectors should be ok unplugged. If you have any worries or live in a high salt area... pack them with some dielectric grease. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||12-01-2001||04:12 PM||||When it's hitting -40º during the DAY, and the bright sun melts the ice... It tends to flow into any space, and freeze solid. My doors were frozen shut this morning... I'm running my keys into the headlight doors before even CONSIDERING flipping them up  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
